! REPRODUCER: VFL STORE ROUNDED (SRD) WAS NOT BUILT -- BUILT 2026-11-16,
! and this deck now passes 5 of 5 and is registered in tests/run_tests.sh
! (bsv/StretchExecute.bsv's ST arm, which SRD shares; decks/vfl_srd.strap
! is the full hand-derived deck).  Cut from Buchholz
! Appendix B, Table B.2 (decks/buchholz_b2_cube_root.strap), whose
! statement at 105.32, "STORE ROUNDED (VB, 12, 1), 8.0, 117", the model
! refuses with Operation Code Invalid:
!
!     StretchCore:   OP site = vflIntOp/unimplemented-code  code=101101 decimal=0
!
! THE SOURCE IS Reference Manual 22-6530-2 printed p.64 (PDF 67),
! "Store Rounded (SRD)", read from the page image:
!
!   "The field from the accumulator is rounded by adding to its
!   magnitude one-half of the proper radix in the position to the right
!   of the offset.  Carries are propagated as in any addition in that
!   radix.  This rounded field replaces the addressed storage field.
!   If a signed operation is specified, the contents of the accumulator
!   sign byte register replace the rightmost byte of the storage field
!   after the sign bit has been modified by the negative sign modifier.
!   The contents of the accumulator and the sign byte register are not
!   changed by this operation.  The accumulator sign is ignored in the
!   actual rounding process."
!
!   Radix: "If a binary operation is specified, a binary one is added
!   to the field from the accumulator in the bit position immediately
!   to the right of the specified offset."
!
! THREE CASES, each expectation worked from those sentences:
!
!   round_up     accumulator (16)00AB80 at offset 0.  SRD(BU,8,8) at
!                offset 8 takes the eight bits left of offset 8, AB;
!                the bit immediately right of the offset is the 1 of
!                80, so AB + 1 = AC.  The target word starts as
!                (16)00FFFFFFFFFFFFFF and only its first byte changes:
!                (16)ACFFFFFFFFFFFFFF.
!   acc_kept     "The contents of the accumulator ... are not changed":
!                a plain ST of the same 24 bits afterwards still stores
!                00AB80: (16)00AB800000000000.     [passes today]
!   round_down   accumulator (16)00AB7F: the bit right of the offset is
!                0, AB stays AB: (16)ABFFFFFFFFFFFFFF.
!   signed_1bit  Table B.2's own shape.  L(B,12,1) at offset 116 of the
!                field (2)00000000101 1 -- magnitude 5, a one-bit sign
!                byte that is minus -- puts 5 in accumulator bits 1-11:
!                bits 0-10 hold 2 and bit 11 holds 1.  SRD(B,12,1) at
!                offset 117 rounds the eleven bits left of offset 117
!                by the bit at 11: 2 + 1 = 3, and the sign byte, minus,
!                is the twelfth bit.  Into the first twelve bits of a
!                word of ones: (2)00000000011 1 = (16)007, so
!                (16)007FFFFFFFFFFFFF.
!
! HOW TO RUN IT (registered in run_tests.sh since SRD was built):
!
!   cd stretch/tests && ./run_program.sh ../decks/repro_vfl_srd.strap \
!       StretchReproVflSrdTb "PC=2014" \
!       "round_up:1010=0xACFFFFFFFFFFFFFF" "acc_kept:1011=0x00AB800000000000" \
!       "round_down:1012=0xABFFFFFFFFFFFFFF" "signed_1bit:1013=0x007FFFFFFFFFFFFF"
!
! OBSERVED 2026-11-15 against the model as it stands: the refusal line
! above printed three times (the first is named, half-word 2002, the
! first SRD); FAILS 3 of 5 -- round_up,
! round_down and signed_1bit each still hold their initial word;
! acc_kept and the PC pass.
! OBSERVED 2026-11-16 with SRD built: ALL TESTS PASSED, 5 of 5.

        SLC, 1000.0
START   L(BU,24,8), VAL
        SRD(BU,8,8), UP, 8
        ST(BU,24,8), KEEP
        L(BU,24,8), VAL2
        SRD(BU,8,8), DOWN, 8
        L(B,12,1), SRC, 116
        SRD(B,12,1), SGN, 117
        BEW, $

        SLC, 1008.0
VAL     DD(BU,24,8), (16)00AB80
VAL2    DD(BU,24,8), (16)00AB7F
SRC     DD(BU,12,8), (2)000000001011

        SLC, 1010.0
UP      DD(BU,64,8), (16)00FFFFFFFFFFFFFF
KEEP    DD(BU,64,8), 0
DOWN    DD(BU,64,8), (16)00FFFFFFFFFFFFFF
SGN     DD(BU,64,8), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF

        END, START
